---
import Baselayout from '../../layouts/base.astro';

const baseUrl = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;

const articleSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Article",
  "headline": "What Is a Gray Water System? A Homeowner's Guide",
  "description": "A gray water system captures wastewater from showers, sinks, and laundry and recycles it for garden irrigation. Learn how it works, what it costs, and how to get one installed.",
  "author": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "Greenwater" },
  "publisher": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "Greenwater", "url": "https://waterwz.com" },
  "mainEntityOfPage": { "@type": "WebPage", "@id": "https://waterwz.com/resources/what-is-gray-water-system/" }
};
---
<Baselayout
  title="What Is a Gray Water System? A Homeowner's Guide"
  description="A gray water system captures wastewater from showers, sinks, and laundry and recycles it for garden irrigation. Learn how it works, what it costs, and how to get one installed."
  canonical="https://waterwz.com/resources/what-is-gray-water-system/"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(articleSchema)} />

  <article class="article-wrap">
    <header class="article-header">
      <a class="back-link" href={`${baseUrl}resources/`}>← Resources</a>
      <p class="eyebrow">Gray Water Guide</p>
      <h1>What Is a Gray Water System? A Homeowner's Guide</h1>
      <p class="article-lead">Gray water systems let homeowners recycle water that would otherwise go straight to the sewer. Here's everything you need to know before deciding if one is right for your home.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="article-body">
      <h2>What is gray water?</h2>
      <p>Gray water (also spelled greywater) is wastewater generated by everyday household activities that don't involve toilets. Showers, bathroom sinks, bathtubs, and laundry machines all produce gray water. It may contain soap, skin cells, and trace contaminants, but it is not contaminated with sewage or fecal matter — which is what makes it safe to recycle.</p>
      <p>By contrast, black water is the wastewater that comes from toilets. Black water requires full sewage treatment and should never be reused at home. A gray water system only touches gray water lines — black water lines stay completely separate and untouched.</p>

      <h2>How does a gray water system work?</h2>
      <p>A gray water recycling system intercepts wastewater from your shower and sink drain lines before it reaches the municipal sewer. The water then passes through a filtration process — typically multiple stages — to remove solids, bacteria, and contaminants. Once filtered, the clean gray water is stored or directed immediately to your garden irrigation lines.</p>
      <p>Modern systems like Greenwater use sensor-driven AI to monitor water quality at every stage of filtration. The system reads real-time quality data and adjusts valve positions and filtration parameters automatically, so every drop that reaches your garden meets quality thresholds — without any manual intervention from you.</p>

      <h2>What can gray water be used for?</h2>
      <p>Properly filtered gray water is safe for:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Garden and lawn irrigation</li>
        <li>Drip irrigation systems</li>
        <li>Watering trees, shrubs, and ornamental plants</li>
        <li>Subsurface irrigation (most common and lowest-risk)</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Gray water should not be used for drinking, cooking, or any application where it might be ingested. Most regulations prohibit using gray water on vegetables that are eaten raw — though Greenwater's filtration system exceeds the standards required for vegetable garden irrigation in most jurisdictions.</p>

      <h2>How much water can a gray water system save?</h2>
      <p>The average household produces between 30 and 50 gallons of gray water per day from showers and sinks alone. Over the course of a year, that adds up to between 10,000 and 25,000 gallons — water that would otherwise be treated and discharged rather than put to use in your garden. Greenwater's system is designed to capture and recycle up to 25,000 gallons per year per household.</p>

      <h2>Does a gray water system require permits?</h2>
      <p>Gray water regulations vary by state and municipality. Many US states — including California, Arizona, and Texas — have adopted simplified gray water codes that allow basic systems without permits. Greenwater's installation connects to existing drain lines without modifying your plumbing stack, which keeps it out of the permit-required category in most jurisdictions. We verify legality for your specific address before every install.</p>

      <h2>Is a gray water system worth it?</h2>
      <p>If your household waters a garden or lawn regularly, a gray water system pays for itself through reduced water bills and reduced reliance on municipal water during droughts or restrictions. Beyond the economics, recycling gray water reduces the load on wastewater treatment infrastructure — a meaningful environmental benefit at scale.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="article-cta">
      <h2>Ready to install a gray water system?</h2>
      <p>Join the Greenwater pilot program and have a system installed in your home in under a day.</p>
      <a href={`${baseUrl}contact/`} class="btn-primary">Talk To The Team</a>
    </div>
  </article>

  <style>
    .article-wrap {
      max-width: 760px;
      margin: 6rem auto 4rem;
      padding: 0 2.5rem;
    }

    .back-link {
      display: inline-block;
      font-size: 0.85rem;
      font-weight: 600;
      color: var(--gw-rust);
      text-decoration: none;
      margin-bottom: 2rem;
    }

    .eyebrow {
      font-size: 0.78rem;
      letter-spacing: 0.12em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--gw-rust);
      font-weight: 700;
      margin: 0 0 1rem;
    }

    .article-header h1 {
      font-family: 'DM Serif Display', serif;
      font-size: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3rem);
      line-height: 1.1;
      color: var(--gw-forest);
      margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
    }

    .article-lead {
      font-size: 1.15rem;
      line-height: 1.65;
      color: var(--gw-text-muted);
      margin: 0 0 3rem;
      border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(26, 46, 26, 0.1);
      padding-bottom: 3rem;
    }

    .article-body h2 {
      font-family: 'DM Serif Display', serif;
      font-size: 1.5rem;
      color: var(--gw-forest);
      margin: 2.5rem 0 0.75rem;
    }

    .article-body p, .article-body ul {
      font-size: 1rem;
      line-height: 1.75;
      color: var(--gw-text-muted);
      margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
    }

    .article-body ul {
      padding-left: 1.5rem;
    }

    .article-body li {
      margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
    }

    .article-cta {
      margin-top: 4rem;
      padding: 2.5rem;
      background: var(--gw-forest);
      border-radius: 1rem;
      text-align: center;
    }

    .article-cta h2 {
      font-family: 'DM Serif Display', serif;
      font-size: 1.8rem;
      color: #fff;
      margin: 0 auto 0.75rem;
    }

    .article-cta p {
      color: rgba(255,255,255,0.65);
      margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
    }

    .btn-primary {
      text-decoration: none;
      background: var(--gw-rust);
      color: #fff;
      padding: 0.9rem 2rem;
      border-radius: 100px;
      font-size: 1rem;
      font-weight: 600;
      display: inline-block;
      transition: transform 0.25s, box-shadow 0.25s;
    }

    .btn-primary:hover {
      transform: translateY(-2px);
      box-shadow: 0 8px 24px rgba(196, 96, 58, 0.3);
      color: #fff;
    }

    @media (max-width: 768px) {
      .article-wrap {
        padding: 0 1.25rem;
        margin-top: 5rem;
      }
    }
  </style>
</Baselayout>
